<p>NASA launched Artemis II from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on April 1, sending astronauts Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover, Christina Koch and Canadian Space Agency astronaut Jeremy Hansen on a roughly 10-day mission around the Moon and back. The flight is the first crewed mission toward the Moon since Apollo 17 in 1972. Orion is expected to loop around the Moon without landing, then return to Earth after testing systems needed for later lunar landings. The mission is a major step in NASA’s Artemis program, which aims to return astronauts to the lunar surface later this decade and eventually support missions to Mars. The crew also marks several milestones noted across coverage: Koch is set to become the first woman on a lunar mission, Glover the first person of color on one, and Hansen the first non-American to take part in a lunar mission, signaling a more internationally representative era of spaceflight.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/-5ONW5eGOE1X9amaAo_VYVm_TzLnu_YAmBUr4acSCgIKwIGeQr5Orh9YPmc2D-ulRrkYi6llOtBqQMdS1bbJmyto2OHUqAwtAhCp7eF475FK' alt='Nasa’s Artemis II Space Launch System rocket carrying the Orion spacecraft lifts off from Launch Complex 39B at Kennedy Space Centre on April 1, 2026 in Cape Canaveral, Florida.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Launch timing: After delays and technical issues before liftoff, the rocket launched at about 6:35 p.m. EDT from Launch Complex 39B, and the crew safely reached orbit.</li><li>Mission profile: The astronauts are expected to spend about four days traveling outward, pass behind the Moon’s far side around April 6, then head back to Earth in Orion.</li><li>Program goals: NASA officials described Artemis II as a dress rehearsal for later missions, including ones that could support a moon base and a sustained human presence on the lunar surface.</li><li>Global support: Australia is tracking Orion with Southern Launch’s TALON telemetry dish at Koonibba, echoing the country’s support role during Apollo 11 in 1969.</li></ul><blockquote>Let&#x27;s go to the moon! - Reid Wiseman</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>NASA leadership: NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman described the launch as an opening act for later missions, including the possible construction of a moon base to support a lasting human presence on the surface. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1987778/nasa-launches-4-astronauts-on-worlds-first-crewed-lunar-mission-in-half-a-century'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>BBC analysis: BBC reporting from Kennedy Space Center highlighted the emotional public response, describing giddy euphoria around a mission framed as serving all humanity after years of delays and anticipation. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cz90yp7w104o'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Semafor: Semafor framed the launch as part of a renewed space race, linking the mission’s scientific and symbolic value to growing geopolitical competition in lunar exploration. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/01/2026/nasas-historic-moon-mission-heralds-new-space-race'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>US senator: In Al Jazeera’s video report, one US senator contrasted the moon mission with US military action against Iran, presenting the launch as a different expression of national priorities. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/1/nasa-moon-mission-a-contrast-to-us-war-on-iran-says-us-senator'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/reactions-as-artemis-ii-astronauts-lift-off-on-historic-lunar-mission'>Reactions as Artemis II astronauts lift off on historic lunar mission</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/sci-tech/science/nasa-artemis-2-launch-live-updates/article70811479.ece'>NASA Artemis II Launch highlights: Artemis II astronauts reach orbit on historic mission to moon and back</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/nasa-s-artemis-ii-rocket-lifts-off-for-the-moon/a-76631089'>NASA&#x27;s Artemis II r